1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,985
NASA's next Moon mission was supposed to

2
00:00:01,985 --> 00:00:02,914
land here.

3
00:00:02,914 --> 00:00:05,087
Now it won't even leave Earth orbit.

4
00:00:05,087 --> 00:00:07,955
In April, four astronauts flew around the

5
00:00:07,955 --> 00:00:09,754
Moon on Artemis Two.

6
00:00:09,754 --> 00:00:11,565
And the next crew's rocket won't even

7
00:00:11,565 --> 00:00:14,015
carry the stage that sends Orion toward

8
00:00:14,015 --> 00:00:15,106
the Moon.

9
00:00:15,106 --> 00:00:16,418
In its place:

10
00:00:16,418 --> 00:00:19,276
a metal barrel with no engine.

11
00:00:19,276 --> 00:00:21,795
NASA made this change back in February,

12
00:00:21,795 --> 00:00:24,872
and in October it published the full plan:

13
00:00:24,872 --> 00:00:27,658
fourteen days in orbit, two Moon landers,

14
00:00:27,658 --> 00:00:29,783
and one test that has to work before

15
00:00:29,783 --> 00:00:32,786
anyone lands near the lunar South Pole.

16
00:00:32,883 --> 00:00:35,263
Here's what they'll actually do up there,

17
00:00:35,263 --> 00:00:37,689
and why the landing now waits for Artemis

18
00:00:37,689 --> 00:00:38,666
Four.

19
00:00:38,666 --> 00:00:41,487
Artemis Two's flight took ten days:

20
00:00:41,487 --> 00:00:44,448
launch on April first, a seven-hour lunar

21
00:00:44,448 --> 00:00:47,246
flyby on April sixth, splashdown off

22
00:00:47,246 --> 00:00:49,591
California on April tenth.

23
00:00:49,591 --> 00:00:51,774
But by then the plan for the next mission

24
00:00:51,774 --> 00:00:53,709
had already changed.

25
00:00:54,223 --> 00:00:56,847
On February twenty-seventh, NASA rewrote

26
00:00:56,847 --> 00:00:59,274
the order of the Artemis program.

27
00:00:59,274 --> 00:01:01,747
Artemis Three had been the landing.

28
00:01:01,747 --> 00:01:03,674
It became a test flight in low Earth

29
00:01:03,674 --> 00:01:06,217
orbit, set for twenty twenty-seven, and

30
00:01:06,217 --> 00:01:09,061
the first landing moved to Artemis Four,

31
00:01:09,061 --> 00:01:11,395
targeted for twenty twenty-eight.

32
00:01:11,395 --> 00:01:14,147
NASA also promised at least one landing

33
00:01:14,147 --> 00:01:16,155
every year after that.

34
00:01:16,155 --> 00:01:19,197
NASA's reasoning, in its own announcement:

35
00:01:19,197 --> 00:01:21,461
keep the rocket and spacecraft as close as

36
00:01:21,461 --> 00:01:23,945
possible to the version that already flew,

37
00:01:23,945 --> 00:01:27,057
fly more often, and build up step by step,

38
00:01:27,057 --> 00:01:28,775
with each mission big enough to make

39
00:01:28,775 --> 00:01:31,643
progress but not so big that it takes

40
00:01:31,643 --> 00:01:33,694
unnecessary risk.

41
00:01:33,698 --> 00:01:35,718
NASA's administrator said that's how the

42
00:01:35,718 --> 00:01:38,831
agency did it in nineteen sixty-nine.

43
00:01:38,831 --> 00:01:41,022
And this isn't a paper mission.

44
00:01:41,118 --> 00:01:43,429
The four main engines for Artemis Three

45
00:01:43,429 --> 00:01:46,041
reached Kennedy Space Center in July.

46
00:01:46,041 --> 00:01:48,746
The Orion capsule and its service module

47
00:01:48,746 --> 00:01:51,134
were joined in late July.

48
00:01:51,486 --> 00:01:53,796
On September thirtieth, technicians put

49
00:01:53,796 --> 00:01:56,037
the nose cones on the twin boosters,

50
00:01:56,037 --> 00:01:57,798
finishing the stack.

51
00:01:57,894 --> 00:01:59,822
More than two hundred and fifty parts from

52
00:01:59,822 --> 00:02:02,504
Artemis Two's Orion are being reused on

53
00:02:02,504 --> 00:02:05,824
later missions, and NASA says reused parts

54
00:02:05,824 --> 00:02:08,216
move some installation work up by about

55
00:02:08,216 --> 00:02:09,458
three months.

56
00:02:09,458 --> 00:02:11,351
Even the launch tower is being turned

57
00:02:11,351 --> 00:02:13,464
around for its next flight nearly fifty

58
00:02:13,464 --> 00:02:15,019
percent faster.

59
00:02:15,019 --> 00:02:16,970
And that missing upper stage?

60
00:02:16,970 --> 00:02:18,328
It isn't scrapped.

61
00:02:18,328 --> 00:02:21,080
NASA reassigned it to Artemis Four, the

62
00:02:21,080 --> 00:02:22,972
mission that actually has to reach the

63
00:02:22,972 --> 00:02:23,949
Moon.

64
00:02:23,949 --> 00:02:27,223
The crew is Randy Bresnik, Andre Douglas

65
00:02:27,223 --> 00:02:30,126
and Frank Rubio of NASA, and Luca

66
00:02:30,126 --> 00:02:33,500
Parmitano of the European Space Agency.

67
00:02:33,597 --> 00:02:35,745
In September they trained with Navy

68
00:02:35,745 --> 00:02:38,937
helicopter crews off San Diego, practicing

69
00:02:38,937 --> 00:02:40,726
being hoisted out of the water after

70
00:02:40,726 --> 00:02:42,254
splashdown.

71
00:02:42,258 --> 00:02:44,092
They've drilled escaping a capsule

72
00:02:44,092 --> 00:02:46,264
underwater, trained in their orange

73
00:02:46,264 --> 00:02:48,887
survival suits, and tasted nearly two

74
00:02:48,887 --> 00:02:50,977
hundred items of space food.

75
00:02:50,977 --> 00:02:52,649
Their mission will be about two weeks

76
00:02:52,649 --> 00:02:54,658
long, and it will never get farther than a

77
00:02:54,658 --> 00:02:56,900
few hundred miles from home.

78
00:02:56,900 --> 00:02:59,477
Artemis Three needs three launches.

79
00:02:59,477 --> 00:03:01,974
First, Blue Origin launches a test version

80
00:03:01,974 --> 00:03:03,471
of its Blue Moon lander.

81
00:03:03,471 --> 00:03:05,364
It can wait in orbit for up to thirty

82
00:03:05,364 --> 00:03:06,304
days.

83
00:03:06,304 --> 00:03:08,719
Then the crew launches on NASA's SLS

84
00:03:08,719 --> 00:03:10,275
rocket from Florida.

85
00:03:10,275 --> 00:03:12,608
Starship goes up later, after the crew is

86
00:03:12,608 --> 00:03:14,536
already in orbit, because it's built for a

87
00:03:14,536 --> 00:03:16,440
much shorter stay in space than Blue

88
00:03:16,440 --> 00:03:17,962
Origin's lander.

89
00:03:17,962 --> 00:03:19,889
The orbits are precise.

90
00:03:19,889 --> 00:03:21,979
The Blue Moon test article circles about

91
00:03:21,979 --> 00:03:24,347
two hundred seventy-three miles up.

92
00:03:24,347 --> 00:03:27,285
Orion starts in a long, stretched orbit,

93
00:03:27,285 --> 00:03:29,514
from eighteen to more than eleven hundred

94
00:03:29,514 --> 00:03:31,755
miles, then rounds it out near three

95
00:03:31,755 --> 00:03:33,484
hundred forty-five.

96
00:03:33,484 --> 00:03:36,120
Starship is planned lower still, at two

97
00:03:36,120 --> 00:03:39,011
hundred six nautical miles, an orbit NASA

98
00:03:39,011 --> 00:03:41,333
picked for communications coverage.

99
00:03:41,333 --> 00:03:43,492
Every number here comes from NASA's flight

100
00:03:43,492 --> 00:03:44,736
profile.

101
00:03:44,736 --> 00:03:46,199
First docking:

102
00:03:46,199 --> 00:03:48,591
Orion pulls up alongside the Blue Moon

103
00:03:48,591 --> 00:03:49,450
lander.

104
00:03:49,450 --> 00:03:51,946
While they're joined, Orion's own software

105
00:03:51,946 --> 00:03:53,966
flies both spacecraft.

106
00:03:53,966 --> 00:03:55,394
Then the hatch opens.

107
00:03:55,394 --> 00:03:57,461
Up to two astronauts, in their orange

108
00:03:57,461 --> 00:04:00,630
survival suits, float inside.

109
00:04:00,630 --> 00:04:03,068
NASA calls it its first evaluation of a

110
00:04:03,068 --> 00:04:05,390
commercial lunar lander with a crew.

111
00:04:05,390 --> 00:04:07,480
They spend about two days checking its

112
00:04:07,480 --> 00:04:09,964
cabin, controls and life support.

113
00:04:09,964 --> 00:04:12,031
Riding along is a dummy the weight of a

114
00:04:12,031 --> 00:04:14,957
spacesuit, wired with sensors, reporting

115
00:04:14,957 --> 00:04:17,408
what the cabin really feels like.

116
00:04:17,408 --> 00:04:19,053
Second docking:

117
00:04:19,057 --> 00:04:20,508
Starship.

118
00:04:20,508 --> 00:04:22,691
The test vehicle is a hundred seventy-one

119
00:04:22,691 --> 00:04:25,570
feet tall, about fifty-two meters, with a

120
00:04:25,570 --> 00:04:27,683
docking port in its nose.

121
00:04:27,683 --> 00:04:30,423
This time Orion uses its forward docking

122
00:04:30,423 --> 00:04:33,244
port and meets Starship nose to nose, a

123
00:04:33,244 --> 00:04:35,636
different geometry from the first docking,

124
00:04:35,636 --> 00:04:38,039
and SpaceX flies the pair.

125
00:04:38,039 --> 00:04:40,372
It lasts up to two hours, and nobody goes

126
00:04:40,372 --> 00:04:41,452
inside.

127
00:04:41,452 --> 00:04:44,250
NASA and SpaceX are still deciding exactly

128
00:04:44,250 --> 00:04:46,619
which control and communication tests to

129
00:04:46,619 --> 00:04:47,572
run.

130
00:04:47,572 --> 00:04:50,091
After Starship, NASA has set aside up to

131
00:04:50,091 --> 00:04:52,646
five more days for extra tests:

132
00:04:52,646 --> 00:04:55,002
more checks of the crew's survival suits,

133
00:04:55,002 --> 00:04:57,615
docking sensor tests, sound and heat

134
00:04:57,615 --> 00:04:59,960
measurements, and studies of how the crew

135
00:04:59,960 --> 00:05:02,189
lives and works on board.

136
00:05:02,189 --> 00:05:05,509
Then, after about fourteen days in total,

137
00:05:05,509 --> 00:05:08,226
Orion comes home and splashes down in the

138
00:05:08,226 --> 00:05:11,244
Pacific, off the coast of California, the

139
00:05:11,244 --> 00:05:13,566
same ocean where Artemis Two landed in

140
00:05:13,566 --> 00:05:14,811
April.

141
00:05:14,811 --> 00:05:17,528
So why spend a whole mission on this?

142
00:05:17,528 --> 00:05:19,676
NASA's Artemis program manager put it

143
00:05:19,676 --> 00:05:20,697
plainly:

144
00:05:20,697 --> 00:05:22,880
every piece of the mission in Earth orbit

145
00:05:22,880 --> 00:05:25,353
is designed to reduce risk before

146
00:05:25,353 --> 00:05:27,779
astronauts go back to the Moon.

147
00:05:27,779 --> 00:05:29,765
Three spacecraft on three different

148
00:05:29,765 --> 00:05:32,377
rockets, working together, which he says

149
00:05:32,377 --> 00:05:34,270
NASA has never attempted.

150
00:05:34,270 --> 00:05:37,021
And on Artemis Four, the landing depends

151
00:05:37,021 --> 00:05:38,774
on exactly this step.

152
00:05:38,774 --> 00:05:40,783
Orion can't land.

153
00:05:40,783 --> 00:05:43,314
A commercial lander has to meet it, take

154
00:05:43,314 --> 00:05:45,972
astronauts down near the South Pole, and

155
00:05:45,972 --> 00:05:47,559
bring them back.

156
00:05:47,563 --> 00:05:49,606
So the first test of this handoff happens

157
00:05:49,606 --> 00:05:52,313
close to home, in Earth orbit.

158
00:05:52,313 --> 00:05:54,658
If this sounds familiar, it should.

159
00:05:54,658 --> 00:05:57,340
In March nineteen sixty-nine, Apollo Nine

160
00:05:57,340 --> 00:06:00,185
tested the lunar module in Earth orbit.

161
00:06:00,185 --> 00:06:03,298
Four months later, Apollo Eleven landed.

162
00:06:03,298 --> 00:06:04,772
What we don't know yet:

163
00:06:04,772 --> 00:06:06,096
a launch date.

164
00:06:06,096 --> 00:06:08,383
NASA says twenty twenty-seven, and nothing

165
00:06:08,383 --> 00:06:09,567
more precise.

166
00:06:09,567 --> 00:06:11,646
The rocket's fueling test at the pad is

167
00:06:11,646 --> 00:06:12,540
still ahead.

168
00:06:12,540 --> 00:06:14,594
And NASA hasn't said which lander will

169
00:06:14,594 --> 00:06:17,487
carry the first crew down on Artemis Four.

170
00:06:17,487 --> 00:06:20,285
So the next Moon mission stays close to

171
00:06:20,285 --> 00:06:22,131
home, on purpose.

172
00:06:22,131 --> 00:06:24,801
If both dockings work, the following crew

173
00:06:24,801 --> 00:06:26,566
goes all the way down.

174
00:06:26,566 --> 00:06:28,505
Artemis Four is the landing.

175
00:06:28,505 --> 00:06:31,647
Artemis Three is the test it can't skip.

